    "https://jobs.latrobe.edu.au/en/job/571012/knowledge-translation-officer": {
      "title": "Knowledge Translation Officer",
      "description": "Knowledge Translation Officer Job No.: 571012 Work type: Fixed Term HEO7 Full-time 12-month contract with the possibility of extension La Trobe University’s Melbourne (Bundoora) campus location Hybrid working arrangements About us The Australian Research Centre in Sex, Health and Society (ARCSHS) works to create and translate knowledge that enables inclusion, equity, health and wellbeing, particularly as they relate to sex, gender, sexuality and relationships. ARCSHS is home to the largest program of LGBTIQA+ health and wellbeing research and knowledge translation in Australia, with expertise in family, domestic and sexual violence, alcohol and other drug use, youth, ageing and mental health. Rainbow Health Australia (RHA) is a program within ARCSHS that supports LGBTIQA+ health and wellbeing through research and knowledge translation, training, resources, policy advice and service accreditation through the Rainbow Tick. About the position Join RHA and contribute to improving LGBTIQA+ health, wellbeing and inclusion across health and community services. As the Knowledge Translation Officer, you will support the development and delivery of RHA’s capability-building and knowledge translation programs, including Rainbow Tick and national primary care initiatives. You will translate research and evidence into practical resources, training and implementation supports; contribute to quality improvement activities; and work with partner organisations to strengthen inclusive and affirmative practice. We are seeking an experienced professional with expertise in training and resource development, strong research and knowledge translation skills, and a demonstrated understanding of LGBTIQA+ health and wellbeing. You will bring excellent facilitation, communication and program administration skills and the ability to work collaboratively across research, health and community service settings. Essential Criteria Skills and knowledge required for the position Demonstrated excellent research and analytical skills, including literature reviews, and the production of key content summaries A relevant degree with four years of post-qualification experience with demonstrated extension of theories and principles; or six years of relevant industry experience; or an equivalent alternate combination of relevant knowledge, training and/or experience Experience in the design and delivery of training programs, including formal qualifications or high-level experience in curriculum development, delivery and management of training programs preferably in the health and human services sector Demonstrated understanding of key issues in LGBTIQA+ health and wellbeing, and preferably experience working in projects aimed at advancing LGBTIQA+ inclusion. Please see the position description for all other Duties and Skills and knowledge required for the position Benefits: • 17% employer contributed superannuation • On site child care facilities • Flexible work arrangements, job-share applicants will be individually considered • Discounts for staff and their family members to study a range of La Trobe courses Why La Trobe: Help transform the lives of students, partners and communities now and in the future Develop your career at an innovative, global university where you’ll collaborate with community and industry to create impact. Enjoy working on our inspiring and stunning campuses – the perfect hub for industry, students and academics How to apply Closing date: By 11:55 pm Monday, 19 th of October Position Enquiries: Jami Jones, Programme Lead, Rainbow Health Australia Jami.Jones@latrobe.edu.au Recruitment Enquiries: Madeleine Parker, First Nations Talent Acquisition Consultant m.parker@latrobe.edu.au Position Description below: KT Officer PD Please submit an online application ONLY and include the following documents: Cover letter addressing your suitability for the position Separate document responding to each key selection criteria and An up to date resume; Job share applications will be considered. Please scroll down to apply. All La Trobe University employees are bound by the Working with Children Act 2005 . If you are successful, you will be required to hold a valid Victorian Employee Working with Children Check prior to commencement . Based Violence (GBV Code). This aligns with our mission to create safe and respectful communities. Candidates will be asked during the recruitment process to declare whether they have ever been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, gender-based violence in previous employment and/or in legal proceedings and provide relevant information to assist in determining suitability. Gender-based violence means any form of physical or non-physical violence, harassment, abuse or threats based on gender, that results in, or is likely to result in harm, coercion, control, fear or deprivation liberty or autonomy. Advertised: 29/9/2026 Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Back to search results Apply now"
    },
    "https://jobs.latrobe.edu.au/en/job/570843/malnuda-barra-yina-family-facilitator": {
      "title": "Malnuda Barra Yina Family Facilitator",
      "description": "Malnuda Barra Yina Family Facilitator Job No.: 570843 Work type: Fixed Term Part-Time .10FTE (approximately half a day a week) Fixed Term 3 years Shepparton Campus Location Classification: Level A - Base salary from $84,205 + 17% superannuation Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12(1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). About the position Based at La Trobe University’s Shepparton Campus, the project will run for more than three years and will explore the important contribution Yorta Yorta people made to the Goulburn Valley fruit-picking industry. Family Facilitators will play an important role in ensuring that the stories, experiences and histories of Yorta Yorta families are captured, respected and shared in a culturally safe way. Each Family Facilitator will represent their own family or clan and will work approximately half a day per week. Family Facilitators must be a member of the Yorta Yorta community and supported by their family or clan to undertake the role. About the Project Malnuda Burra Yina: Gabra – Stories of Yorta Yorta Fruit Picking between 1920 to 1970 will document and explore the experiences and contributions of Yorta Yorta families involved in the Goulburn Valley fruit-picking industry between 1920 and 1970. The project will work with individual families and clans to capture their unique histories, stories and experiences, including oral histories, photographs and other family records. Skills and Experience To be considered for this position, you will have: A recognised connection to the Yorta Yorta community and endorsement from your family/clan to take on this role Cultural knowledge of Yorta Yorta history and ways of doing, including protocols and family structures, with the ability to apply these in a project engagement setting Knowledge of Yorta Yorta community history, cultural expectations, and how families/clans operate, or experience applying these in community settings A commitment to handling your family’s stories, photos, oral histories, and other materials with confidentiality, cultural sensitivity, and respect Please refer to the Position Description for other duties, skills and experience required for this position. Position description: Family Facilitators Position Description Benefits 17% superannuation Indigenous Identified Allowance Private Health Insurance discount How to apply Closing date: Wednesday the 21 st of October 2026 Position Enquiries: Neville Atkinson, Adjunct Professor, Indigenous Strategy & Education Email: N.Atkinson@latrobe.edu.au Recruitment Enquiries: Madeleine Parker – First Nations Talent Acquisition Consultant, Email: m.parker@latrobe.edu.au Please submit an online application ONLY and must include the following documents: An up-to-date resume; 1 page cover letter addressing your interest and suitability for the position. Please note, this is an identified Indigenous position and in accordance with La Trobe University policies and procedures confirmation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander heritage will be required as part of the application. Only candidates with Full Working Rights and residing in Australia may apply for this position. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We welcome and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Our commitment to diversity and inclusion La Trobe University is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive workforce. We take an intersectional approach by actively supporting and encouraging people of all backgrounds and abilities to submit an application and aim to ensure that the recruitment and employee experience is as accessible and inclusive as possible. Flexibility in interview format will be offered to shortlisted candidates. Our commitment to safety All La Trobe University employees are bound by the Working with Children Act 2005 . If you are successful, you will be required to hold a valid Victorian Employee Working with Children Check prior to commencement. National Code on Gender Based Violence in Higher Education La Trobe University is committed to upholding the National Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence (GBV Code). This aligns with our mission to create safe and respectful communities. Candidates will be asked during the recruitment process to declare whether they have ever been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, gender-based violence in previous employment and/or in legal proceedings and provide relevant information to assist in determining suitability. Gender-based violence means any form of physical or non-physical violence, harassment, abuse or threats based on gender, that results in, or is likely to result in harm, coercion, control, fear or deprivation liberty or autonomy. Advertised: 22/9/2026 Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Back to search results Apply now"
    },
    "https://jobs.latrobe.edu.au/en/job/570919/malnuda-barra-yina-research-assistant": {
      "title": "Malnuda Barra Yina Research Assistant",
      "description": "Malnuda Barra Yina Research Assistant Job No.: 570919 Work type: Fixed Term Part-Time .40FTE (2 days per week), Fixed Term 3 years Shepparton Campus Location Classification: Level A - Base salary from $84,205 + 17% superannuation Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12(1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). About the position This is an exciting project and is Indigenous led with many of the researchers being Yorta Yorta descent. The team includes up to 15 families/clans from the Goulburn Valley region. The research will record and explore the individual family history of each family/clan’s involvement in the fruit picking industry. In addition to supporting research activities, the Research Assistant will contribute to community engagement, project coordination and storytelling activities across the Malnuda Burra Yina project. This includes supporting podcast production, assisting Family Facilitators and researchers, coordinating engagement activities, and providing flexible project support as priorities emerge throughout the life of the project. The Research Assistant will be situated in the Gabra Biik Wurruwila Wutja Research Centre (Shepparton Campus). The Research Assistant will be directed by the Malnuda Barra Yina Research Steering Committee and reports to the Director of the Gabra Biik, Wurruwila Wutja Indigenous Research Centre. The Research Assistant will be supported in their role with skilling up or training where required and throughout the project. Our ideal candidate needs to demonstrate they can work in a team environment and work independently and collaboratively as part of the team. The candidate needs to be organised to prioritise and meet project deadlines. Skills and Experience To be considered for this position, you will have: Knowledge of the community, expertise in working with community and acceptance of the Yorta Yorta community to work with families Acknowledge and support compliance with university policies, research ethics requirements, and funding body obligations. Ability to build credible relationships with Aboriginal individuals and communities, showing respect for cultural values and protocols working successfully with Yorta Yorta Community members in formal settings, such a Yorta Yorta organisation, a higher education organisation, a gallery, museum, or archive. Please refer to the position description for other duties, skills and experience required for this position. Position description: PD-Level-A Malnuda Research Assistant Benefits 17% superannuation Indigenous Identified Allowance Private Health Insurance discount Plus, many more, click HERE How to apply Closing date: Wednesday the 21 st of October 2026 Position Enquiries: Neville Atkinson, Adjunct Professor, Indigenous Strategy & Education Email: N.Atkinson@latrobe.edu.au Recruitment Enquiries: Madeleine Parker – First Nations Talent Acquisition Consultant, Email: m.parker@latrobe.edu.au Please submit an online application ONLY and must include the following documents: An up to date resume; 1 page cover letter addressing your interest and suitability. Please note, this is an identified Indigenous position and in accordance with La Trobe University policies and procedures confirmation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander heritage will be required as part of the application. Only candidates with Full Working Rights and residing in Australia may apply for this position. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We welcome and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Our commitment to diversity and inclusion La Trobe University is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive workforce. We take an intersectional approach by actively supporting and encouraging people of all backgrounds and abilities to submit an application and aim to ensure that the recruitment and employee experience is as accessible and inclusive as possible. Flexibility in interview format will be offered to shortlisted candidates. Our commitment to safety All La Trobe University employees are bound by the Working with Children Act 2005 . If you are successful, you will be required to hold a valid Victorian Employee Working with Children Check prior to commencement. National Code on Gender Based Violence in Higher Education La Trobe University is committed to upholding the National Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence (GBV Code). This aligns with our mission to create safe and respectful communities. Candidates will be asked during the recruitment process to declare whether they have ever been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, gender-based violence in previous employment and/or in legal proceedings and provide relevant information to assist in determining suitability. Gender-based violence means any form of physical or non-physical violence, harassment, abuse or threats based on gender, that results in, or is likely to result in harm, coercion, control, fear or deprivation liberty or autonomy. Advertised: 22/9/2026 Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Back to search results Apply now"
    },
    "https://jobs.latrobe.edu.au/en/job/571019/lecturer-mathematics": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Mathematics",
      "description": "Lecturer, Mathematics Job No.: 571019 Work type: Fixed Term Full-time 12-month Fixed Term Contract Level B T&R position La Trobe Bundoora Campus location Make a difference in a position that offers great job satisfaction About the position This position is located within the Department of Mathematical and Physical Sciences, part of the School of Computing, Engineering and Mathematical Sciences at La Trobe University’s Melbourne (Bundoora) Campus. The School delivers innovative undergraduate and postgraduate programs across computing, engineering and mathematical sciences, supported by a strong research profile and four research centres spanning AI, technology, medical innovation, and materials science. Skills and Experience PhD in applied mathematics or applied statistics. Demonstrated competence in teaching and curriculum development. Evidence of high quality and/or high impact research conducted and published or otherwise disseminated, relative to opportunity. Sound analytical skills with an ability to communicate complex information clearly both verbally and in writing. High level organisational skills with the ability to set priorities, meet deadlines, initiate and follow-up actions, all with minimal or no supervision. Please refer to the Position Description for other duties, skills and experience required for this position. Welcome to Bundoora campus – Please click on the video link below: https://f.io/KDo0ceng Benefits: 17% employer contributed superannuation On site child care facilities Flexible work arrangements Discounts for staff and their family members to study a range of La Trobe courses How to apply Closing date: By 11:55pm, Monday the 19 th of October 2026 Position Enquiries: Rebecca Chisholm, Associate Professor, Mathematics & Statistics via email R.Chisholm@latrobe.edu.au Recruitment Enquiries: Madeleine Parker, Talent Acquisition Consultant via email m.parker@latrobe.edu.au Position Description below: PD-Level-B-Teaching-and-Research Only candidates with Full Working Rights in Australia may apply for this position. Please submit an online application ONLY and include the following documents: Cover letter addressing your suitability for the position; and A separate document addressing the Key selection criteria located in the PD under the “Essential Criteria Skills and knowledge required for the Position\"; and An up-to-date resume Please scroll down to apply. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We welcome and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. La Trobe University is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive workforce. We take an intersectional approach by actively supporting and encouraging people of all backgrounds and abilities to submit an application and aim to ensure that the recruitment and employee experience is as accessible and inclusive as possible. Flexibility in interview format will be offered to shortlisted candidates. All La Trobe University employees are bound by the Working with Children Act 2005. If you are successful, you will be required to hold a valid Victorian Employee Working with Children Check prior to commencement . Why La Trobe: Develop your career at an innovative, global university where you’ll collaborate with community and industry to create impact. Enjoy working on our inspiring and stunning campuses – the perfect hub for industry, students and academics Help transform the lives of students, partners and communities now and in the future La Trobe University is committed to upholding the National Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence (GBV Code). This aligns with our mission to create safe and respectful communities. Candidates will be asked during the recruitment process to declare whether they have ever been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, gender-based violence in previous employment and/or in legal proceedings and provide relevant information to assist in determining suitability. Gender-based violence means any form of physical or non-physical violence, harassment, abuse or threats based on gender, that results in, or is likely to result in harm, coercion, control, fear or deprivation liberty or autonomy. Advertised: 17/9/2026 Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Back to search results Apply now"
